    "q-344079": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344079",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "A physicist measured two particular features, ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ for each oscillation produced when a mass hanging on a spring is displaced and then released. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular oscillation. \nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}≈[\\begin{aligned}0.456 \\\\ 0.890\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}≈[\\begin{aligned}0.890 \\\\ -0.456\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the physicist wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?",
      "worked_solution": "Recall the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance) corresponds to the *first principal component* of the data.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14198769/assets/f0f0623703d1a3767a7c3efd275e977c6933465faeb761991f00da705bac4a2c.png)\n\n \n\nLet  \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.456 & 0.890 \\\\ 0.890 & -0.456\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nbe the matrix of principal components. \n\nThen, applying the change of variables $Y=XV,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are uncorrelated), and\n\n- the features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in ${y}_{1}$).\n\nApplying our change of variables to a single observation $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.456 & 0.890 \\\\ 0.890 & -0.456\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.456{x}_{1}+0.890{x}_{2} & 0.890{x}_{1}-0.456{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that ${y}_{1}=0.456{x}_{1}+0.890{x}_{2}$ will contain most of the variance of the data. \n\nTherefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=0.456{x}_{1}+0.890{x}_{2}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
